<section class="block">
<h2>Who this is for</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Founders and scale-ups</strong> in Riyadh, Jeddah and the Eastern Province who need senior technical leadership.</li>
<li><strong>Enterprises and family groups</strong> modernising core systems and setting an AI strategy.</li>
<li><strong>Regional companies expanding</strong> between Saudi Arabia, the UAE and the wider GCC.</li>
<li><strong>Investors</strong> backing Saudi technology companies.</li>
</ul>
</section>
<section class="block">
<h2>What shapes technology decisions in Saudi Arabia</h2>
<p>The Personal Data Protection Law has been enforceable since September 2024, administered by the Saudi Data and AI Authority<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. Transfers of personal data outside the Kingdom need an appropriate basis and safeguards, such as standard contractual clauses, with risk assessments required in some cases<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>. For AI systems that often call hosted models abroad, the design question is what data leaves the country, and whether it needs to.</p>
<table class="tbl"><thead><tr><th>Rule or standard</th><th>Who it touches</th><th>What it means for architecture</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>PDPL</td><td>Organisations processing personal data in the Kingdom</td><td>Data mapping, lawful basis, retention and data subject rights designed into platforms</td></tr><tr><td>Transfer regulations</td><td>Anyone sending personal data abroad, including to AI APIs</td><td>In-country processing for sensitive data, minimisation and documented transfer safeguards</td></tr><tr><td>Arabic-first users</td><td>Customer-facing products</td><td>Bilingual retrieval, Arabic normalisation and per-language evaluation</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>Related reading: <a href="/insights/saudi-pdpl-cross-border-ai/">Saudi PDPL and cross-border AI</a>, <a href="/case-studies/arabic-english-bilingual-rag-assistant/">a bilingual Arabic and English assistant</a> and <a href="/insights/private-llm-on-prem/">private LLMs on-prem</a>.</p>
</section>
<section class="block">
<h2>Typical first projects</h2>
<ol>
<li>An AI strategy and architecture review grounded in PDPL and transfer rules.</li>
<li>Arabic and English knowledge assistants with citations and in-country hosting.</li>
<li>Platform modernisation with cloud, data and security foundations.</li>
<li>Technical due diligence for investors.</li>
</ol>
</section>
<section class="block">
<h2>Time zones</h2>
<p>Riyadh is one hour behind the UAE, so Saudi teams share my full working day, including the Sunday to Thursday week where needed.</p>
</section>
<section class="block">
<h2>How engagements work</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Architecture and AI readiness review</strong> (2 to 4 weeks): a clear picture of the current platform, risks and a prioritised roadmap.</li>
<li><strong>Fractional CTO</strong> (ongoing, part-time): technical strategy, architecture governance, hiring and board-level communication.</li>
<li><strong>Build and hand-off</strong>: hands-on delivery of a platform or AI capability, documented so your team can run it.</li>
<li><strong>Technical due diligence</strong>: an independent view of a target's technology for investors and acquirers.</li>
</ul>
<p>Every engagement starts with a free 30-minute call. See <a href="/how-i-work/">how I work</a> for the full process.</p>
</section>
